00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:36.320
Imagine you allocated your full TFSA contribution last week into a single sector ETF after a

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.420
strong earning season.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:43.260
Your order filled at the prevailing price, but the real mechanics involve how diversification

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:49.480
across asset classes actually reduces portfolio volatility without lowering expected returns.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.620
Modern portfolio theory shows that combining equities, bonds, and alternatives

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.280
moves the portfolio toward the efficient frontier where risk drops for the same return level.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:08.440
Strategic allocation sets long-term weights such as 60% equities and 40% bonds, while tactical shifts

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:15.820
respond to valuation signals like the current TSX trading near 5% below highs. Canadian examples

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:25.100
include all equity ETFs such as XEQT for younger investors, or balanced options like VGRO and XBAL

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:32.740
that automatically rebalance. Bond's still provide ballast post-2022 because their correlation with stocks

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:40.100
can turn negative during equity drawdowns. Age-based rules like 100-Age and equities oversimplify,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:45.940
because they ignore personal risk tolerance and time horizon. Rebalancing on a threshold

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:51.880
basis, such as when an asset class drifts more than 5% from target, outperforms calendar

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:57.760
rebalancing by capturing mean reversion. The common misconception is that adding more stocks

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.680
always increases risk. The fix is to run a simple correlation check on your

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.960
current holdings using free portfolio tools before the next contribution.

